"unesdoc.unesco.org (https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark%3A/48223/pf0000076671)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark:/48223/pf0000076671\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark:/48223/pf0000076671; Total lines: 0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.pc.gov.pk/uploads/vision2025/Pakistan-Vision-2025.pdf)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 110; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.pc.gov.pk/uploads/vision2025/Pakistan-Vision-2025.pdf\",\"lineno\":75}); Total lines: 4976\nL26@P3-4: 100PAKISTAN VISION 2025 1\nL27@P4: www.pc.gov.pk\nL28@P4-5: Introduction2 PAKISTAN VISION 2025\nL29@P5: One Nation - One Vision\nL30@P5: For a nation to progress it must have a clear\nL38@P5: on such a vision\u2014the “Pakistani Dream”, a\nL39@P5: vision of a prosperous, equitable, tolerant,\nL40@P5: and dynamic society\u2014which was at the heart\nL41@P5: of the Independence Movement, even if, over\nL42@P5: the years, its clarity has diminished. It was\nL43@P5: the foundational vision for the new country,\nL44@P5: inspired by that generation of leaders, and\nL45@P5: articulated through the struggles of our people\nL46@P5: for independence and nationhood. The purpose\nL47@P5: of this document and all that has gone into its\nL48@P5: preparation is to recreate this vision, re-build\nL49@P5: upon it, and help translate it into reality.\nL50@P5: Since 1947, Pakistan has made considerable\nL51@P5: progress on many fronts. However, on the\nL52@P5: eve of our 68th independence day, there is\nL53@P5: consensus that the pace of progress has not\nL54@P5: been commensurate with the promise and\nL55@P5: potential of our nation; we need to do better\nL56@P5: and faster. There is a general misconception\nL57@P5: that faster means no or less planning and\nL58@P5: more action. The reality is that the quality of\nL59@P5: planning is one of the key determinants of\nL60@P5: the speed of action and desired outcomes.\nL61@P5: Over time, the nature and role of planning\nL62@P5: has undergone a major transformation.\nL63@P5: Today the national planning process is more\nL64@P5: participatory, collaborative, people and\nL65@P5: market oriented. Plans succeed when they\nL66@P5: manifest the aspirations of a nation, empower\nL67@P5: the citizens, and especially the private sector,\nL68@P5-6: Our Shared DestinationPAKISTAN VISION 2025 3\nL69@P6: www.pc.gov.pk\nL70@P6: to play their respective roles, provide equal\nL71@P6: equitable opportunities to all and assign a very\nL72@P6: well defined role to government as a supporter,\nL73@P6: facilitator, regulator and performance driven\nL74@P6: service provider.\nL75@P6: Pakistan Vision 2025 is designed to represent\nL76@P6: an aspirational destination. It will serve as\nL77@P6: a critical guide-post for the development of\nL78@P6: an effective strategy and road-map to reach\nL79@P6: our national goals and aspirations. It is not\nL80@P6: meant to represent the resultant strategy and\nL81@P6: programme itself. The Vision will be realized\nL82@P6: through strategies and programmes defined in\nL83@P6: associated five-year and annual plans.\nL84@P6: Pakistan today faces formidable social,\nL85@P6: economic, security and governance challenges.\nL86@P6: Many nations have faced similar challenges\nL87@P6: in history and successfully turned them\nL88@P6: into opportunities through sound economic\nL89@P6: planning, good governance and consistency\nL90@P6: in policy implementation. We believe that,\nL91@P6: once effectively addressed, our challenges\nL92@P6: likewise offer unprecedented opportunities\nL93@P6: for transformational progress. As we pass\nL94@P6: through an era of unprecedented change and\nL95@P6: complexity, it is imperative that we refresh\nL96@P6: our framework for national development.\nL97@P6: A renewed commitment to the founding\nL98@P6: vision is needed, both to address the current\nL99@P6: challenges and set out realistic and ambitious\nL100@P6: targets for the future\u2014including ensuring that\nL101@P6: Pakistan succeeds in achieving the proposed\nL102@P6: Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) of\nL103@P6: zero poverty and hunger, universal access to\nL104@P6: health services, education, modern energy\nL105@P6: services, clean water and sanitation, and join\nL106@P6: the league of Upper Middle Income countries\nL107@P6: by 2025. Our ultimate aspiration is to see\nL108@P6: Pakistan among the ten largest economies of\nL109@P6: the world by 2047 – the centennial year of our\nL110@P6: independence.\nL111@P6: Pakistan is currently facing serious challenges\nL112@P6: on various fronts. These include the\nL113@P6: combination of low growth and high inflation,\nL114@P6: which is one of the major factors leading to the\nL115@P6: perpetuation of poverty and unemployment.\nL116@P6: Energy shortages have posed great problems\nL117@P6: to the citizens as well as businesses and\nL118@P6: agriculture. Social indicators reflect serious\nL119@P6: deficiencies in education, health and\nL120@P6: population, gender equity and social services.\nL121@P6: The law and order situation in the country\nL122@P6: poses a critical threat to security as well as the\nL123@P6: economy. The decade-long struggle against\nL124@P6: terrorism and extremism continues to impose\nL125@P6: immense social, economic, and human costs.\nL126@P6: The challenges are compounded by a number of\nL127@P6: adverse exogenous developments, especially\nL128@P6: the looming threat of climate change, the\nL129@P6: aftermath of global economic, financial, and\nL130@P6: energy crises, unabated fragility of the global\nL131@P6: financial system, and the continued stagnation\nL132@P6: in developed country import demand as well\nL133@P6: as aid flows.\nL134@P6: The situation calls for a consensus-based\nL135@P6: national vision and comprehensive strategy\nL136@P6: to not only combat these challenges, but also\nL137@P6: proactively embrace the future based on ‘The\nL138@P6: Pakistani Dream’, which lay at the heart of the\nL139@P6: movement for the creation of Pakistan.\nL140@P6: Our ultimate destination\nL141@P6: is to see Pakistan\nL142@P6: among the ten largest\nL143@P6: economies of the\nL144@P6: world by 2047 – the\nL145@P6: centennial year of our\nL146@P6-7: independence.4 PAKISTAN VISION 2025\nL147@P7: One Nation - One Vision\nL148@P7: Notwithstanding the multitude of challenges,\nL149@P7: recent developments, both internal\nL150@P7: and external, also provide a basis for\nL151@P7: optimism. Recent years have witnessed the\nL152@P7: transformational impact of the deepening of\nL153@P7: democratic culture and institutions. There is\nL154@P7: a growing consensus that the future of the\nL155@P7: country is linked with the upholding of the\nL156@P7: principles of the rule of law, independence of\nL157@P7: the judiciary and constitutionalism. The space\nL158@P7: for freedom of expression through the growing\nL159@P7: voice of a vibrant media has expanded. In\nL160@P7: spite of attacks on journalists, the resolve of\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.pc.gov.pk/uploads/vision2025/Pakistan-Vision-2025.pdf)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 110; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.pc.gov.pk/uploads/vision2025/Pakistan-Vision-2025.pdf\",\"lineno\":1450}); Total lines: 4976\nL1409@P33: where the disadvantaged and marginalized\nL1410@P33: are reached, their basic needs met, and their\nL1411@P33: access to economic opportunities improved.\nL1412@P33: Social justice facilitates and promotes\nL1413@P33: upward social mobility. Our focus will be on\nL1414@P33: creating a caring society, where everyone\nL1415@P33: shares the dividends of prosperity and\nL1416@P33: development. Without social justice the gains\nL1417@P33: from development are lost due to resulting\nL1418@P33-34: inequality, exploitation and conflict.PAKISTAN VISION 2025 31\nL1419@P34: www.pc.gov.pk\nL1420@P34-35: Pillar I32 PAKISTAN VISION 2025\nL1421@P35: One Nation - One Vision\nL1422@P35: Pakistan Vision 2025 seeks a society\nL1423@P35: in which every citizen has the\nL1424@P35: opportunity to transform their quality\nL1425@P35: of life. This requires strengthening\nL1426@P35: human and social capital, which will\nL1427@P35: allow the population to optimally\nL1428@P35: contribute to and effectively benefit\nL1429@P35: from economic growth. Pakistan\nL1430@P35: lags far behind its peers in areas\nL1431@P35: like education, health and social\nL1432@P35: development. The UNDP Human\nL1433@P35: Development Report 2013 has\nL1434@P35: ranked Pakistan at 146th out of 187\nL1435@P35: countries on the HDI ranking, which\nL1436@P35: measures health, education and\nL1437@P35: standard of living.\nL1438@P35: Putting People First:\nL1439@P35: Developing Human and\nL1440@P35-36: Social CapitalPAKISTAN VISION 2025 33\nL1441@P36: www.pc.gov.pk\nL1442@P36: Overcoming this deficit is the foremost priority\nL1443@P36: of Vision 2025. Since human and social capital\nL1444@P36: development is a prerequisite for all other\nL1445@P36: development it is the very first of our seven\nL1446@P36: pillars. Recognizing the size and scale of this\nL1447@P36: endeavour, we conceive a very significant\nL1448@P36: increase in resource allocation, and quantum\nL1449@P36: improvement in the quality of service delivery\nL1450@P36: through good governance and innovation.\nL1451@P36: Pakistan Vision 2025 ‘Performance Assurance\nL1452@P36: Units’ for each of the areas identified below will\nL1453@P36: be formed to provide oversight and monitor\nL1454@P36: progress. These units will be part of the ‘Pakistan\nL1455@P36: Vision 2025 Secretariat’, as referred to in the\nL1456@P36: concluding chapter.\nL1457@P36: Education\nL1458@P36: Pakistan Vision 2025 aims at substantial\nL1459@P36: expansion in levels of education as well as\nL1460@P36: improvements in the quality of education. We\nL1461@P36: target public expenditure on education to reach\nL1462@P36: 4% of GDP by 2018.\nL1463@P36: Pakistan ranks 113th out of 120 countries\nL1464@P36: in UNESCO’s Education for All Education\nL1465@P36: Development Index. Pakistan’s literacy rate (57%)\nL1466@P36: lags well behind the country’s neighbours. This\nL1467@P36: proportion also includes those who could only\nL1468@P36: write their names. Literacy rate in rural areas of\nL1469@P36: Pakistan is even lower at 50%. Pakistan has low\nL1470@P36: net enrolment ratios at all three levels – primary,\nL1471@P36: secondary and tertiary – with a much lower\nL1472@P36: female enrolment rate as compared to males.\nL1473@P36: When we move from primary to secondary and\nL1474@P36: tertiary levels, enrolment ratios decrease sharply.\nL1475@P36: Pakistan experiences high pupil-teacher ratios,\nL1476@P36: 40 at the primary level and 42 at the secondary\nL1477@P36: level. Pakistan has the world’s second highest\nL1478@P36: out-of-school population of children. Public\nL1479@P36: sector expenditure on education is barely 2% of\nL1480@P36: GDP. This is compounded by insufficient trained\nL1481@P36: teachers and their absenteeism, and weak\nL1482@P36: governance resulting in the poor quality of public\nL1483@P36: schooling.\nL1484@P36: Education development will be led by the\nL1485@P36: provinces as it is a devolved function, however\nL1486@P36-37: the Federal Government will play the role of a 34 PAKISTAN VISION 2025\nL1487@P37: One Nation - One Vision\nL1488@P37: catalyst in partnership with the provinces to\nL1489@P37: transform the education system in the country.\nL1490@P37: with support from the federal government.\nL1491@P37: Government at all levels will ensure that\nL1492@P37: individuals are able to pursue their economic,\nL1493@P37: social, and intellectual objectives in the best\nL1494@P37: possible way. The provincial governments are\nL1495@P37: committed to achievement of the Millennium\nL1496@P37: Development Goals for education. In order to\nL1497@P37: achieve these goals the provincial governments\nL1498@P37: are determined to increase their education\nL1499@P37: budgets significantly. The Federal Government\nL1500@P37: will not only help them in achieving MDGs at\nL1501@P37: provincial levels but also perform an active role\nL1502@P37: in reducing educational inequities across the\nL1503@P37: four provinces. This will increase geographic\nL1504@P37: and economic mobility within the country thus\nL1505@P37: strengthening national spirit and solidarity.\nL1506@P37: Comprehensive reforms, comprising of detailed\nL1507@P37: actions in curriculum, pedagogy, technology,\nL1508@P37: governance, assessment as well as social\nL1509@P37: and economic relevance will be made in the\nL1510@P37: educational system to improve the quality of\nL1511@P37: public schooling. These reforms will ensure\nL1512@P37: that the educational system helps individuals in\nL1513@P37: acquiring/sharpening of creative and analytical\nL1514@P37: abilities and problem solving skills, inculcates\nL1515@P37: ethics and values, creates the appreciation of civil\nL1516@P37: rights and civic responsibilities, promotes health\nL1517@P37: and well-being, and encourages the pursuit\nL1518@P37: of economic prosperity. They aim to increase\nL1519@P37: curiosity, critical thinking and innovation\nL1520@P37: thus shifting the focus of education from\nL1521@P37: memorization to critical learning and creating a\nL1522@P37: bright future through enterprise, creativity and\nL1523@P37: ethics. This will align the educational system\nL1524@P37: with the national development agenda.\nL1525@P37: Co-curricular activities will be encouraged in\nL1526@P37: schools to produce well rounded individuals. The\nL1527@P37: relevance of the curriculum to our development\nL1528@P37: needs will be improved and efforts will be\nL1529@P37: made to reduce the dropout rate. Modernized\nL1530@P37: teaching methods will be adopted, including the\n"
